WEBVTT

NOTE This transcript is the episode script as written and fact-checked, timed to the recording. Timestamps are approximate within a sentence.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.600
What's up guys, welcome back to CrimeTimeSnacks.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:15.000
A hotel maintenance worker investigating complaints of low water pressure found a missing tourist floating inside a large rooftop cistern.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.000
That discovery happened on February 19, 2013, at the Stay on Main hotel in downtown Los Angeles.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.400
Guests had actually been complaining about a weird taste coming from the tap water.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.100
The maintenance worker, Santiago Lopez, made the discovery.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:33.700
According to a statement by the chief of the Los Angeles Fire Department, the tank had to be drained completely.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:37.800
Then crews had to cut it open from the side just to remove her five-foot-four frame.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.600
The body in the water belonged to 21-year-old Elisa Lam.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.300
Elisa was a Canadian tourist on a solo trip around the West Coast.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:49.000
She was originally from Vancouver, where she was a student at the University of British Columbia.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.500
Her family had been wary of her traveling by herself.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:56.500
As a compromise, she made sure to check in with her parents every single day to let them know she was safe.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:02.300
Before she became the center of a massive mystery, she was just a young woman documenting her life online.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:05.600
In mid-2010, she started a blog on Blogspot called Ether Fields.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:12.700
She posted photos of models in fashionable clothing, but she also wrote honestly about her daily life and her struggles with mental illness.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:16.600
She used a quote from the novelist Chuck Palahniuk as an epigraph for the site.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:23.700
In a January 2012 post, she used that quote for the title, writing about being haunted by the idea that you are wasting your life.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:28.700
She lamented that a relapse at the start of the school term had forced her to drop several classes.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:33.600
She worried her transcript would look suspicious and keep her from attending graduate school.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.500
She wrote that she felt utterly directionless and lost.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:43.700
A little over two years after starting that blog, she announced she was abandoning it for a Tumblr she called Nouvelle-Nouveau.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:48.900
The content there shifted mostly to fashion photos and quotes, with very few posts in her own words.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:52.700
But she kept that exact same Chuck Palahniuk quotation as the epigraph.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:58.700
It was a digital footprint of a student looking for her place in the world, right before she arrived in Los Angeles.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:02.900
Elisa’s writing about feeling lost wasn't just typical college burnout.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:05.800
The case file shows a very specific medical background.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.100
She was formally diagnosed with bipolar disorder and depression.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:13.400
To manage her mental health, she was prescribed a complex rotation of medications.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.600
The medical history lists several specific drugs.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:22.000
Her doctors had her on bupropion, lamotrigine, quetiapine, dextroamphetamine, and venlafaxine.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:28.300
Balancing antidepressants, mood stabilizers, and anti-psychotics requires strict adherence to work correctly.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:36.200
Medical experts note that using antidepressants to treat bipolar disorder can actually risk inducing manic side effects if done without caution.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.600
Her family reportedly kept her mental illness private.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.700
They stated she had absolutely no history of suicidal ideations or attempts.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:47.700
But the records do show a documented history of medication non-compliance.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.400
Elisa had a pattern of not taking her bipolar medications as prescribed.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.900
When she went off her medication, the consequences were severe.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:58.800
The records show that she suffered from intense hallucinations during these periods.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.600
The paranoia would get so bad that she would physically hide under her bed for refuge.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:04.800
These were not mild side effects.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.800
They were full psychiatric emergencies.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:11.400
According to the background files, she was hospitalized at least once for one of these episodes.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.600
Another report claimed she had even gone missing for a brief period during a past crisis.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.000
This is what the evidence actually shows about her baseline.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.900
She was a young woman managing a severe psychiatric condition.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.900
It is a vital piece of the puzzle to remember before we look at her arrival in California.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:27.900
So let's look at that trip.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:32.700
Elisa traveled down the West Coast alone, riding on Amtrak trains and intercity buses.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:35.900
She made a stop at the San Diego Zoo and posted photos online.

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:38.500
Her ultimate goal was to make it up to Santa Cruz.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:41.000
But on January 26, she arrived in Los Angeles.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:44.100
Two days later, she checked into the Cecil Hotel.

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:46.700
The building sits right near downtown's Skid Row.

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:50.700
At first, she was booked into a hostel-style shared room on the fifth floor.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:52.800
But that arrangement did not last long.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:56.700
Her new roommates started complaining to the staff about her actions.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:59.500
The hotel's lawyer later called it certain odd behavior.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:05.100
According to Amy Price, who managed the hotel at the time, Elisa started leaving notes for the other girls in the room.

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:07.100
The notes told them to go home and go away.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:08.900
It escalated from there.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:14.600
Elisa began locking the door to the shared room and demanding a password before she would let anyone inside.

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:18.700
After just two days of this, management moved her into a private room of her own.

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:22.000
The strange behavior was not contained to the hotel.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:26.600
A few days before she vanished, Elisa went to Burbank to attend a live taping of the Conan show.

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:28.200
She never got to see the end of it.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:32.000
Security escorted her off the premises because she was being disruptive.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.500
The cracks were starting to show, right in public.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:39.500
We just saw how her behavior escalated at the comedy taping and inside her shared room.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:43.700
But the case file shows this trip was originally supposed to be a simple getaway.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:47.700
Elisa wanted a break from her studies at the University of British Columbia.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:54.100
We already tracked her route on those intercity buses from the San Diego Zoo up to her arrival in Los Angeles on January 26.

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:57.600
She was blogging about her encounters and self-discovery along the way.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.300
Her ultimate destination was supposed to be Santa Cruz.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.800
Let's talk about where she actually ended up staying.

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:07.900
The Cecil Hotel was an old building with a notoriously heavy reputation.

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:11.400
It had long been a magnet for darkness in downtown Los Angeles.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:14.800
Over the decades, the property was home to serial killers.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.200
It was the site of numerous murders and suicides.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:22.300
Elisa could not have known she was walking into one of the most infamous addresses in American history.

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:24.500
She never made it to Santa Cruz.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:29.000
January 31 was the day she was scheduled to check out of the Cecil and leave for Santa Cruz.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:32.900
We know she spent time around then at a nearby shop called The Last Bookstore.

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:39.000
Katie Orphan, the manager of The Last Bookstore, was the only person outside the hotel who recalled seeing her that day.

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:43.200
Orphan later told a local CBS station that Elisa was buying books and music.

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:45.900
She was getting gifts for her family back in Vancouver.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:50.800
The shop owner noted that Elisa clearly had plans to return home and reconnect with her relatives.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:54.100
That makes what happened next even harder to explain.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:06:00.300
Elisa contacted her parents in British Columbia daily while she was traveling, right up until the day she disappeared.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.400
On January 31, they did not hear from her.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:06.800
When that daily check-in did not happen, her family knew something was wrong.

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:09.400
They reached out to the Los Angeles Police Department.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:14.200
We already established the shift in her demeanor during those early days at the hotel.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:16.800
That was the original plan for her stay in Los Angeles.

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:20.000
We also talked about how that arrangement quickly fell apart.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.200
She was leaving them notes telling them to go away.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.600
That is exactly why hotel management stepped in.

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:28.800
We also know this was not just happening behind closed doors.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:31.300
Security had to escort her out for disruptive behavior.

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:34.700
When they couldn't find her, they turned to the public for help.

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:38.200
They released surveillance footage taken from one of the hotel's elevators.

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:40.700
It was published six days before her body was discovered.

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:44.900
That four-minute video of her elevator episode changed the entire trajectory of this case.

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:48.900
The video shows Elisa acting erratically on the day she disappeared.

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:52.800
You can see her stepping into the elevator and pushing all the floor buttons.

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:53.900
The doors never close.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:57.900
She steps in and out of the car, poking her head out sideways toward the hallways.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:00.600
She peers out a few times before stepping out entirely.

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:05.400
In the final minutes of the footage, she is standing by the left side of the open door.

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:09.000
She starts moving her hands in random, unnerving gestures in the air.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:10.900
Nobody else is captured on the video.

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:12.100
She is entirely alone.

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:14.500
That footage went completely viral.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:18.600
It crossed international borders, drawing massive attention in Canada and China.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:20.500
It racked up tens of millions of views.

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:26.400
Internet sleuths started dissecting every frame, spawning theories about murder conspiracies and even evil spirits.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:29.400
The reality of the case file is far more grounded.

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:37.300
When her body was finally recovered from that rooftop tank, she was floating lifelessly next to clothing similar to what she was wearing in that elevator video.

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:41.300
Hotel staff told authorities she was always seen by herself around the premises.

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:44.500
The autopsy results only added to the confusion.

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:48.400
The coroner's office ultimately ruled her death an accidental drowning.

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:52.500
But they found absolutely no signs of foul play from the evidence processed.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:57.500
The toxicology report is where the medical history we discussed earlier becomes critical.

00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:02.800
The screening confirmed there was a very small quantity of alcohol, but no illegal substances in her system.

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:05.400
It did show the presence of her prescription drugs.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:11.500
One summary of her toxicology report was posted online by a Reddit sleuth with an obvious interest in medicine.

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:15.400
The summary showed she took at least one antidepressant the day she vanished.

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:20.300
She had taken a second antidepressant and a mood stabilizer recently, but not that specific day.

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:25.600
Most importantly, the report indicated she had not taken her anti-psychotic medication recently at all.

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:28.600
This brings us right back to her baseline.

00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:33.600
The record shows a young woman whose medication regimen was completely out of balance.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:38.000
As NBC LA reporter Lolita Lopez put it, we know the who, what, when, and where.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:39.900
But the why is always the question.

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:44.100
We already know January 31 was supposed to be her last day at the Cecil Hotel.

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:47.000
She was scheduled to check out and head north to Santa Cruz.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:52.200
Before leaving, she made that stop at The Last Bookstore to pick up gifts for her family in British Columbia.

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:58.300
The manager of that store, Katie Orphan, gave a few more details to CNN about that specific interaction.

00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:02.500
She described the young student as outgoing, very lively, and very friendly.

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:05.600
It was a completely normal, pleasant retail encounter.

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:09.800
The case file shows Elisa was actually worried about the weight of her luggage.

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:15.400
Orphan recalled her debating whether the books she wanted would be too heavy to carry around as she continued her travels.

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:19.100
That is the behavior of someone actively planning the next leg of their journey.

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:24.500
This interaction stands in stark contrast to the erratic actions her roommates reported just days earlier.

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:28.300
She was lucid, friendly, and looking forward to handing those gifts to her parents.

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:31.000
But that evening, the daily phone call home never happened.

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:35.300
Her parents knew immediately that this silence was a massive red flag.

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:38.100
They did not wait around for her to turn up in Santa Cruz.

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:41.500
They booked flights to California to join the search themselves.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.900
The LAPD started their investigation right at the Cecil Hotel.

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:49.200
Officers searched her private room and brought in tracking dogs to sweep the massive building.

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:52.300
The official timeline notes those dogs even went up to the rooftop.

00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:56.300
The search dogs were completely unsuccessful in detecting her scent up there.

00:09:56.300 --> 00:10:01.000
Sergeant Rudy Lopez later explained that officers legally searched the hotel to the extent that they could.

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:03.400
They did not search every single room in the building.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:10.200
Lopez stated they could only enter other guest rooms if they had probable cause to believe a crime had been committed.

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:12.500
So the rest of the hotel was left untouched.

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:16.000
A full week went by with absolutely no sign of the missing student.

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:20.100
On February 6, the police decided they needed to escalate their efforts.

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:24.200
They printed flyers with her face and plastered them around the downtown neighborhood.

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:27.200
They also posted her image online to drum up public awareness.

00:10:27.700 --> 00:10:30.400
That decision brought the local media into the fold.

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:33.200
It took the case out of the hotel and into the public eye.

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:36.900
The search for a missing tourist was about to become a global obsession.

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:40.700
We already established that Elisa missed her scheduled check-in call.

00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:44.800
When her parents did not hear from her, they contacted the Los Angeles Police Department.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:47.300
They did not just wait by the phone in British Columbia.

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:49.800
The family flew to Los Angeles to help with the search.

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:52.400
They were living through the worst moment of their lives.

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:57.600
Years later, documentary makers noted that the family refused to participate in television shows about the case.

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:03.900
They did not want to deal with narratives that exaggerated the circumstances of the tragedy or claimed an evil presence took control of her.

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:05.800
They just wanted to find their daughter.

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:09.200
The police started their physical search inside the hotel.

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:13.200
They went through Elisa's room looking for any clues about where she might have gone.

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:16.300
But the building was massive, and they were restricted by the law.

00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:19.100
The Cecil was set right in the heart of downtown Los Angeles.

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:22.200
That specific neighborhood has a massive homelessness problem.

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:24.000
The hotel itself had a split purpose.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:26.800
Some floors were reserved for visiting tourists like Elisa.

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:31.700
Other floors were utilized as short or long-term accommodation for people living in the Skid Row area.

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:35.200
The police were dealing with a property filled with permanent residents.

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:39.000
Sergeant Rudy Lopez went on the record about those limitations.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:41.700
They could not just kick down every door in the building.

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:43.700
That legal wall frustrated the public.

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:47.700
Fingers were immediately pointed at the long-term residents and the hotel staff.

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:52.400
Former hotel manager Amy Price later called those accusations stressful and heartbreaking.

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:56.800
Price stated she genuinely thought Elisa had simply gotten mixed up with the wrong crowd.

00:11:56.800 --> 00:11:59.800
She noted that would not be hard to do in downtown Los Angeles.

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:03.500
Because of those legal guardrails, the physical search was limited.

00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:06.800
The dogs were led through the building and eventually taken up to the roof.

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:10.100
That roof was a sprawling space housing the hotel's massive water tanks.

00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:13.200
That rooftop sweep turned up absolutely nothing.

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:15.400
The dogs failed to detect her scent up there.

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:19.300
The immediate search of the premises yielded no trace of the missing student.

00:12:19.300 --> 00:12:22.400
The lack of evidence left a massive void in the investigation.

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:25.300
Web sleuths stepped in and developed severe tunnel vision.

00:12:25.300 --> 00:12:30.000
They focused on strange coincidences, like a tuberculosis test literally named Lam-Elisa.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:33.400
They compared the case to the plot of a horror movie called Dark Water.

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:37.400
But those ghost stories and conspiracies did not help the actual investigation.

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:40.300
The fact remained that the physical search hit a dead end.

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:44.700
The physical search of that massive hotel went as far as the law allowed.

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:49.900
Police searched Elisa's room, and they sent dogs through the building, including the rooftop.

00:12:49.900 --> 00:12:51.700
But they did not search every room.

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:57.100
Without probable cause to believe a crime had been committed, they could not push any further inside.

00:12:57.700 --> 00:13:01.100
That legal barrier meant a week went by without a single major break.

00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:04.200
By February 6, the LAPD knew they could not solve this quietly.

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:10.200
They took Elisa's face to the streets, hanging flyers across the neighborhood and pushing her image onto the internet.

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:12.600
That decision changed everything.

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:16.000
The investigation was no longer confined to the walls of the Cecil.

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:22.100
The media took hold of those missing person flyers, and the search for the young student became a very public obsession.

00:13:22.700 --> 00:13:25.400
We already talked about that pixelated elevator video.

00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:29.500
They officially released that security camera footage to the public on February 13.

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:31.600
Investigators needed a break in the case.

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:36.800
But when the LAPD put that elevator clip online, it went further than asking the community for tips.

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:40.000
It brought massive public and media attention to the case overnight.

00:13:40.700 --> 00:13:43.000
The footage immediately went viral online.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:47.500
It was reposted widely across different platforms and crossed international borders.

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:50.400
It was uploaded to the Chinese video-sharing site Youku.

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:56.100
In just its first ten days on that site, the clip accumulated three million views and forty thousand comments.

00:13:56.600 --> 00:13:59.000
People found the video deeply unsettling to watch.

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:05.700
The internet started tearing the footage apart, and viewers quickly argued that the video had been tampered with before being made public.

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:08.400
They pointed to a few specific details to make their case.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:12.500
Online sleuths claimed that parts of the clip had been intentionally slowed down.

00:14:12.500 --> 00:14:15.900
They also pointed out that the timestamp on the video was completely obscured.

00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:19.300
Most importantly, they argued that nearly a minute of footage had been removed.

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:24.600
These amateur investigators theorized the edits were made to protect someone's identity.

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:30.000
They believed someone else was in that hallway, either related to the disappearance or just a bystander.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.800
The missing time fueled a massive wave of speculation.

00:14:33.400 --> 00:14:36.600
Other theories emerged to explain her unusual moves and gestures.

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:41.500
Some viewers suggested she was trying to get the elevator car to move to escape from someone pursuing her.

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:45.300
Others guessed she might be under the influence of ecstasy or another party drug.

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:49.800
We already know the later toxicology results ruled out any illegal substances.

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:53.200
But in mid-February, the public did not have those medical records.

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:58.500
They just had a strange video that blurred the line between a real human tragedy and an internet ghost story.

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:02.700
The release of that footage changed the entire scale of the investigation.

00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:07.400
Millions of people were now watching the Cecil Hotel, waiting to see what the police would find next.

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:10.300
You already know the search ended on February 19.

00:15:10.300 --> 00:15:13.000
Guests were also complaining about low water pressure.

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:15.900
But the case file shows the situation was actually much worse.

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:20.000
People staying at the Stay on Main reported that the water was coming out colored black.

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:23.300
There were also complaints about flooding in parts of the building.

00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:26.500
That is what finally sent him up to the roof that morning.

